From: mjb-is <mjb-is@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:47:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 084/120] directorybackup: -no-mtime leaves real times out of
 the archive

Every file's and folder's modification time is written into the PXAR,
so a time that changes changes the archive bytes and the chunks around
that entry are uploaded again although nothing in them changed. A store
that files its data under hash-named folders (aa/bb/<rest of the hash>)
keeps touching its folders and so re-uploads much of the archive every
run.

-no-mtime ("no-mtime": true in a JSON config) records 0 (1970-01-01) as
the time of every file and folder, in the archive entries and in the
catalog, so only content changes cost new chunks. Off by default, CLI
only. The real times are not kept: a restore dates every item
1970-01-01 (with the restore fix in this PR a recorded 0 is applied
like any other time).

- PXARArchive.NoMTime; entryMTime gives the time to record for an entry.
- Config.NoMTime and the -no-mtime flag, passed through backup,
  backup_many and backup_real like -novss.
- README: the flag in the list and a short section on when to use it.

Tests (pxar_nomtime_test.go): a tree backed up, its times all moved,
backed up again: with NoMTime the PXAR stream and the catalog are byte
for byte the same, without it both differ (so the check would notice a
leaked time). What NoMTime records reads back as 0 from the archive and
the catalog, and a restore dates files and folders 1970-01-01.
pbscommon tests pass on Windows 10 and Linux (Mint 21.3, Go 1.25),
directorybackup tests on Windows; vet clean and directorybackup builds
for windows and linux.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JSSBMF6hRk92jsHCMKgDRx

Fork adaptation: noMTime is threaded through the fork's exclusion and
-parallel paths (backup, backup_many, backup_parallel, backup_one,
backup_real) next to excludes; the fork's README is kept.

